WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.612
Reliable services assume that calls can
fail, dependencies can slow down,

2
00:00:03.692 --> 00:00:06.062
callers can send too much work, and
resources are finite.

3
00:00:06.142 --> 00:00:09.797
Security architecture includes resilience
because unavailable or unstable services

4
00:00:09.877 --> 00:00:11.754
can create business risk, operational
risk,

5
00:00:11.834 --> 00:00:14.304
and pressure to bypass normal controls
during an incident.

6
00:00:14.384 --> 00:00:17.001
Go's context patterns are useful for
timeouts, cancellation,

7
00:00:17.081 --> 00:00:19.798
and request-scoped cleanup when they are
applied consistently.

8
00:00:19.878 --> 00:00:23.335
A request context can carry deadlines and
cancellation signals through handlers,

9
00:00:23.415 --> 00:00:25.736
service logic, database calls, and
outbound requests.

10
00:00:25.816 --> 00:00:29.076
The architecture should make it normal for
long-running work to respect those

11
00:00:29.156 --> 00:00:29.551
signals.

12
00:00:29.631 --> 00:00:31.261
Retries should be designed carefully.

13
00:00:31.341 --> 00:00:33.564
A retry can help with a temporary
dependency failure,

14
00:00:33.644 --> 00:00:36.953
but it can also increase load on a
struggling system or repeat side effects if the

15
00:00:37.033 --> 00:00:38.367
operation is not safe to repeat.

16
00:00:38.447 --> 00:00:41.212
Defensive designs consider backoff, retry
limits, idempotency,

17
00:00:41.292 --> 00:00:44.799
and whether a retry belongs at the caller,
service, queue, or infrastructure layer.

18
00:00:44.879 --> 00:00:47.843
Rate limits, request size limits,
connection limits, queue controls,

19
00:00:47.923 --> 00:00:50.590
and backpressure help services remain
available under stress.

20
00:00:50.670 --> 00:00:52.843
These controls do not need to assume
hostile intent.

21
00:00:52.923 --> 00:00:55.985
They are useful for accidental spikes,
misbehaving clients, batch jobs,

22
00:00:56.065 --> 00:00:57.893
dependency failures, and ordinary growth.

23
00:00:57.973 --> 00:01:00.096
Abuse resistance is also resilience
engineering.

24
00:01:00.176 --> 00:01:03.090
Error handling should distinguish expected
failure, invalid input,

25
00:01:03.170 --> 00:01:05.442
dependency failure, and unexpected
internal faults.

26
00:01:05.522 --> 00:01:07.103
Callers need safe, stable responses.

27
00:01:07.183 --> 00:01:08.566
Operators need useful evidence.

28
00:01:08.646 --> 00:01:10.770
Developers need enough context to fix the
problem.

29
00:01:10.850 --> 00:01:14.356
The architecture should avoid leaking
internal details while still preserving the

30
00:01:14.436 --> 00:01:15.918
signals needed for investigation.

31
00:01:15.998 --> 00:01:19.110
Defensive resilience means failing safely,
preserving useful evidence,

32
00:01:19.190 --> 00:01:20.721
and avoiding unnecessary exposure.

33
00:01:20.801 --> 00:01:23.171
A service that times out clearly, cancels
work promptly,

34
00:01:23.251 --> 00:01:24.585
limits unbounded resource use,

35
00:01:24.665 --> 00:01:28.122
and records meaningful failure categories
is easier to operate than one that waits

36
00:01:28.202 --> 00:01:30.820
indefinitely or returns inconsistent
behavior under stress.
